  <article class="almanac-prose">
    <section id="why-one" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.1 · fixed model surface</span>
        <h2>Capability count does not become prompt size.</h2>
        <p>Many agent systems expose one function schema per operation. Dekopon exposes exactly one function—<code>bash</code> with one string field, <code>script</code>—whether the operator grants two capabilities or two hundred.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="authority-state-machine">
        <div><span>model receives</span><strong><code>bash({ script })</code></strong><small>one fixed schema</small></div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">writes a plan →</i>
        <div class="authority-broker-box">
          <span>inside the sandboxed interpreter</span>
          <div><strong><code>cap --list</code></strong><b>→</b><strong>loops + jq</strong><b>→</b><strong>capability calls</strong></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">→ bounded result</i>
        <div><span>model receives</span><strong>output + exit code</strong><small>replayable by an operator</small></div>
      </div>

      <p>A correct model turn can express a multi-step plan in one tool call: discover the available vocabulary, fetch several values, filter structured output, branch on a result, and invoke another capability. That removes repeated model round trips without turning the model into a local shell user.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-decision">
        <strong>“bash” describes the familiar syntax, not the authority.</strong>
        <p><code>dekopon-shell</code> is a native tree-walking interpreter. It never calls <code>/bin/bash</code>, starts a process, opens a file, reads the host environment, or creates a socket.</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="data-path" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.2 · representation map</span>
        <h2>Structure survives the whole plan.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="data-journey-map">
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-remote">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>untrusted model output</span><strong>one tool call</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node"><small>model protocol</small><strong><code>{"script":"..."}</code></strong><code>JSON object required</code></div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ validate exact tool + string argument</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-host">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span><code>dekopon-run</code></span><strong>pure Rust interpreter</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>parser</small><strong>syntax tree</strong><code>rejected forms fail loudly</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ evaluate →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>value model</small><strong><code>serde_json::Value</code></strong><code>arrays + maps + scalars</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ dispatch →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>capability seam</small><strong>direct or broker</strong><code>same outcome enum</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
        <i aria-hidden="true">↓ object input · bounded object/value output</i>
        <div class="journey-zone journey-zone-guest">
          <div class="journey-zone-label"><span>fresh execution boundary</span><strong>Wasm provider or broker proposal</strong></div>
          <div class="journey-node-row">
            <div class="journey-node"><small>input</small><strong>JSON object</strong><code>provider validates fields</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ operation →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>outcome</small><strong>succeeded / failed / denied</strong><code>not flattened</code></div>
            <b aria-hidden="true">→ return →</b>
            <div class="journey-node"><small>pipeline</small><strong>structured value</strong><code>no text remarshal needed</code></div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <p>A pipeline passes one JSON value to the next command, closer to <code>jq</code> than to a Unix byte pipe. A whole-right-hand-side command substitution preserves structure. Capability flags such as <code>--post-id 7</code> become <code>{"postId":7}</code>; repeated flags become arrays. Provider inputs and outputs therefore do not need ad-hoc string parsing between every step.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="language" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.3 · useful subset</span>
        <h2>Keep the planning power. Remove the escape hatches.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="comparison-table-wrap">
        <table class="almanac-table strategy-table">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Available</th><th scope="col">How it behaves</th><th scope="col">What stays absent</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Control flow</th><td><code>if</code>, <code>for</code>, <code>while</code>, <code>until</code>, <code>case</code>, functions, break/continue</td><td>subshells, background jobs, process substitution</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Structured work</th><td>real <code>jq</code>, JSON variables, arrays/maps, arithmetic, structured pipelines</td><td><code>jq env</code>, host clock filters, inherited variables</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Text utilities</th><td><code>grep</code>, <code>sed</code>, <code>cut</code>, <code>sort</code>, <code>uniq</code>, <code>wc</code>, base64, <code>xargs</code></td><td>unbounded regex/glob behavior or silently ignored flags</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Named buffers</th><td><code>&gt;</code>/<code>&gt;&gt;</code> write in-memory values read only by <code>cat</code></td><td>filesystem paths, descriptors, devices, pipes</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Capability access</th><td>bare capability names, <code>cap</code>, optionally capability-backed <code>curl</code></td><td>raw sockets, DNS, subprocess commands, arbitrary host calls</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Clock</th><td><code>date</code> only after explicit <code>--shell-allow-clock</code>, UTC only</td><td>ambient time by default, timezone environment, setting the clock</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>Unsupported dangerous constructs fail by their own name rather than being approximated. <code>eval</code>, <code>exec</code>, <code>source</code>, backticks, arrays pretending to be bash arrays, file-descriptor redirection, and glob-like case patterns cannot make a model believe work happened when it did not. Ordinary glob characters stay inert because there is no filesystem to expand them against.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="dispatch" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.4 · one abstract seam</span>
        <h2>Every outside effect becomes a capability decision.</h2>
      </div>

      <pre><code><span class="code-purple">pub trait</span> CapabilityInvoker {
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> granted(&amp;self) -&gt; Vec&lt;String&gt;;
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> describe(&amp;self, capability: &amp;str) -&gt; Option&lt;CapabilityDescription&gt;;
    <span class="code-purple">fn</span> invoke(
        &amp;self,
        capability: &amp;str,
        input: serde_json::Value,
    ) -&gt; CapabilityCallResult;
}</code></pre>

      <div class="future-topology">
        <article>
          <div><span>FIRST LEG</span>{{ status("current", "Direct") }}</div>
          <h3>Import-free registry</h3>
          <p>If an operator-loaded pure capability exists locally, the runner invokes it in a fresh bounded Store. No authorization or broker audit is implied.</p>
          <strong>Can do</strong><ul><li>pure transforms</li><li>schema-driven computation</li><li>read-only effect label only</li></ul>
        </article>
        <div class="topology-crossing"><span>only when absent locally</span><strong>proposal →</strong><small>no identity fields</small></div>
        <article>
          <div><span>SECOND LEG</span>{{ status("current", "Broker") }}</div>
          <h3>Policy-authorized registry</h3>
          <p>With <code>prompt --broker</code>, capabilities outside direct mode fall through to the separate broker. A denial remains exit code <code>126</code>, not a generic provider error.</p>
          <strong>Can do</strong><ul><li>constrained HTTP</li><li>read or explicit write</li><li>evidence + durable audit</li></ul>
        </article>
      </div>

      <p><code>curl</code> follows the same rule. The builtin only parses familiar flags into <code>{uri, method, headers, body}</code> and submits them to one operator-selected capability. In direct mode that capability cannot load if it imports HTTP. In broker mode, the broker—not the builtin—decides whether any request is allowed.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="budgets" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.5 · resource model</span>
        <h2>No single counter pretends to bound everything.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="invariant-notes">
        <article><span>script</span><h3>Plan bounds</h3><p>Steps, parser nesting, recursion, cumulative value bytes, output bytes/lines, wall time, and capability-call count.</p></article>
        <article><span>session</span><h3>Fan-out bounds</h3><p>At most four tool calls per model turn and one capability-call budget shared across every script in the prompt session.</p></article>
        <article><span>Wasm</span><h3>Guest bounds</h3><p>Fresh Store memory, fuel, input/output bytes, and deadline independently constrain each component operation.</p></article>
        <article><span>broker</span><h3>Effect bounds</h3><p>Concurrency, frame size, I/O deadline, host calls, HTTP bytes, destination, method, and provider output.</p></article>
      </div>

      <p>The separate counters matter. A cheap loop can exhaust steps. Repeated capability calls can stay low in parser steps. Doubling a string can be cheap in operations and expensive in memory. A fast script can invoke a slow provider. Each attack shape has a bound at the layer that actually owns it.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="telemetry" class="article-section">
      <div class="article-heading">
        <span>02.6 · trace shape</span>
        <h2>Observe commands without exporting their arguments.</h2>
      </div>

      <p>Every command word that actually executes becomes one <code>shell.command</code> span beneath <code>prompt.script</code>. It records the owned vocabulary name when safe, resolution kind, argument <em>count</em>, exit code, and outcome. Function names and unknown model-authored words become <code>&lt;withheld&gt;</code>; argument values never become attributes.</p>

      <div class="article-callout callout-plain">
        <strong>Why counts, not argv?</strong>
        <p><code>curl -d '{"apiKey":"..."}'</code> and <code>cap some.id '{"token":"..."}'</code> carry provider input wearing command-line syntax. Recording argv would create a second data-egress path around the broker’s redaction discipline.</p>
      </div>

